    "body": "With `round_robin_gradients`, ZeRO-1/2 lay the flat buffer out in round-robin order, but `_link_all_hp_params` only passed those offsets to `link_hp_params` when there was alignment padding (#8277). Without padding the mapping is summed up in `bit16_groups` order, so after the first reordered parameter everything points at a neighbour's slice. `safe_get_full_fp32_param`, `safe_get_full_grad` and the rest of the fragment API hand back another parameter's data, and universal checkpoints mislabel the fragments. Training itself is fine, it only touches the flat partitions.\n\nI ran into it while working on #8609. On 2×H20, ZeRO-2, six layers:\n\n- `safe_get_full_fp32_param(p)` vs `p`: 0 without round robin, 5.9e-1 with it, 0 with this change.\n- A universal checkpoint saved without round robin and resumed with it (Adam, fp32) was 2.4 off after two steps. With this change it's bit-identical, and so are the other two combinations.\n\nThe fix just always passes the round-robin offsets. The existing fragment tests only check that the getters return something, which is probably why this has been around since tensor fragments came to ZeRO-2 (#2727). The new `TestTensorFragmentValues` compares them against a plain PyTorch copy of the model on the same batch; the round-robin cases fail on master and pass here.\n\nAlso ran `test_zero_tensor_fragment.py`, `test_stage2_flatten_on_gpu.py`, `test_muon_checkpoint.py`, the AutoEP grad parity and unit tests, and the fp32 cases of `test_universal_checkpoint.py`: 328 passed, 33 skipped.\n\n\n## Environment\n\nNVIDIA H20-3e, driver 580.105.08, Python 3.11.15, torch 2.9.1+cu128; the runs above used 2 GPUs.\n",
